\begin{maintheorem}[The oriented diffeomorphism type]
\hypertarget{tag-03KU}{}\label{series:V:D}
\StatementRecord{series:V:D}{proved}{03KU}{\SeriesRef{maps:src:F.3.7}, \SeriesRef{maps:src:F.4.1}}
The period planes extend over the two finite root charts and
the marked cusp, giving a compact complex fourfold diffeomorphic to
$3\mathbb{HP}^2\#(-\mathbb{HP}^2)$.  Its characteristic vector is
carried explicitly to $(1,1,1,1)$, after which the known smooth
classification and homotopy-sphere absorption identify the oriented
manifold.

\end{maintheorem}

\subsection{How the paper is organized}
Part~I (Section~2) constructs the fibre-product fourfold of Theorem~A.  Part~II
constructs the rank-six family: the cusp and the finite fillings and
the integral eight-manifold (Sections~3--5), the periods (Section~6),
the diffeomorphism type (Section~7), the parameter domain (Section~8),
cohomology, deformations and isomorphisms (Section~9), the boundary of
the family (Section~10), the classification of the integral data
(Section~11) and the existence of periods for every such datum
(Section~12).  Part~III treats the shifted fibre products.  The figure
shows which main theorems use which, and the table names the argument
that carries each dependency.
